MAN(1)                         P32W Unix                          MAN(1)

NAME
       man - print pages of the manual

SYNOPSIS
       man [section] name
       man -k word

DESCRIPTION
       man finds the manual page for name and prints it. The pages
       are plain text, already formatted for an 80-column terminal;
       when the standard output is a terminal man shows the page
       through more(1), otherwise it copies it out unchanged, so
       man ls > ls.txt and man sh | grep history both work.

       If section (one of 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or 8) is given, only that
       section is searched. Otherwise the sections are searched in
       the order 1, 8, 2, 3, 4, 5 and the first page found is shown:
       man mkdir shows the command mkdir(1); man 2 mkdir shows the
       system call mkdir(2).

       A page may describe several names (gzip(1) also covers gunzip
       and zcat); any of the names finds it.

       With -k, man prints the entries of the whatis database whose
       NAME line contains word, one per line, as

              gunzip (1) - compress or expand files

       The match ignores case. Use it when you know what you want to
       do but not what the command is called.

OPTIONS
       -k     keyword search of /usr/man/whatis instead of a page.

EXAMPLES
       man ls                  the ls command
       man 2 open              the open system call
       man -k directory        every page about directories
       man intro               an introduction to the manual

FILES
       /usr/man/manS/name.S    the page for name in section S
       /usr/man/whatis         the NAME lines of all pages

EXIT STATUS
       0 if a page (or for -k, an entry) was found, 1 if not.

HELP
       Every program also answers --help, when it is the only argument,
       with its manual page: ls --help is man ls. (The C library's
       start-up does it before main, so programs compiled on P32W get it
       too, if they have a page.)

SEE ALSO
       intro(1), more(1), grep(1)

BUGS
       There is no nroff: the pages are stored formatted and cannot
       be reflowed for a narrower terminal. There is no -a (show all
       matches) and no apropos or whatis command; use man -k.
